## Decorators this program wrote for itself.
##
## A decorator here is not a feature demonstration: each one exists because the
## same three lines were about to be written into four functions. What they have
## in common is that they are about the *answer*, not about the work, which is
## exactly the kind of rule that reads better above a function than inside it.
##
## A `deco` body runs at expansion time, before the program exists, so what a
## closure written here may read is its own parameters, its own `const`s and the
## prelude. Reaching for a name at the top of the file is refused where it is
## written, with `VN2023`, rather than quietly reading as nothing.
module examples.programs.pokedex.shape

## The answer with its first letter raised.
##
## The API names everything in lower case, and every one of them is shown to a
## person. Capitalising at the point of display would mean remembering to, in
## each of the places that display.
pub deco named(target: Fn) {
  target.wrap(fn (call, args) => call(args).capitalize)
}

## `fallback` wherever the answer is nothing.
##
## Half the fields of a Pokemon are optional, and a dex full of `null` is a dex
## nobody can read. The default belongs with the reader, not with each caller.
##
## @param fallback  What to show instead of nothing.
pub deco instead(target: Fn, fallback) {
  target.wrap(fn (call, args) => call(args) ?? fallback)
}

## A list answer, written out as text.
##
## @param separator  What goes between the items.
pub deco joined(target: Fn, separator: string) {
  target.wrap(fn (call, args) => call(args).join(separator))
}
